window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-92522f3829ca4795"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"build-responsive-front-end-designs\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">构建响应式前端设计</h1> <p>将截图和视觉参考转化为响应式 UI，并通过视觉检查验证。</p> <p>使用 Codex 将截图和设计简报转化为代码，使其符合代码库的设计系统；随后使用 Playwright 对照参考图，检查不同屏幕尺寸下的表现并持续迭代。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>从头开始创建新的前端项目。</li> <li>根据截图在现有代码库中实现已经设计好的页面或流程。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"skills-plugins\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">技能与插件</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>技能或插件</th> <th>用途</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/skills/tree/main/skills/.curated/playwright-interactive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">$playwright（需联网）</a></td> <td>在真实浏览器中打开应用，验证实现并迭代布局与交互。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">起步提示词</h2> <p>构建响应式前端设计</p> <p>建议推理级别：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">使用我提供的截图和说明作为事实依据，在当前项目中实现此 UI。\n\n要求：\n- 复用现有设计系统中的组件和设计令牌。\n- 将截图转化为本仓库使用的工具类和组件模式，不要另行发明一套平行系统。\n- 尽可能贴合参考图的间距、布局、层次结构和响应式行为。\n- 遵循仓库现有的路由、状态和数据获取模式。\n- 确保页面适用于桌面和移动设备。\n- 如果截图细节含糊，请选择仍符合整体方向的最简单实现，并简要说明所作假设。\n\n验证：\n- 对照所提供的截图检查完成后的 UI 外观和行为。\n- 使用 $playwright-interactive 检查 UI 是否符合参考，并在需要时持续迭代。\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">技能</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"introduction\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">简介</h2> <p>如果你提供截图、简短的设计简报或一些灵感参考，Codex 可以据此制作响应式 UI，同时遵循项目中已经确立的模式。</p> <p>借助 Playwright 技能，Codex 可以在真实浏览器中打开应用，将实现效果与截图对照，检查不同屏幕尺寸下的表现，并持续迭代布局或交互，让结果逐渐接近目标。</p> <p><a id=\"start-from-references\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">从参考资料开始</h2> <p>提供清晰的参考资料，说明你希望 UI 是什么样子。范围较小的任务可能只需一张截图；但如果能提供多个状态，交接会更好，例如桌面和移动端布局、悬停或选中状态，以及重要的空状态或加载状态。</p> <p>参考资料不一定要是完美的设计资产。只要能把预期层次、间距和方向说清楚，让 Codex 不必猜测就足够了。</p> <p><a id=\"be-specific\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">具体说明</h2> <p>对预期交互模式和风格描述得越具体，结果通常越好。</p> <p>如果参考资料没有明确说明你想要其他风格，模型往往会默认采用常见模式，因此界面可能显得普通。你提供的输入越充分——无论是更多视觉参考，还是更具体的指示——界面越可能呈现鲜明的风格。</p> <p><a id=\"prepare-the-design-system\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">准备设计系统</h2> <p>如果目标代码库已经有清晰的组件层，Codex 可以直接使用现有组件和设计系统，而不是重新创建。</p> <p>如果项目没有使用常见技术栈，可以明确告诉 Codex 应复用哪些基础组件、设计令牌在哪里，以及项目中的按钮、输入框、卡片、字体排版和图标分别以什么为准。</p> <p>如果从现有代码库开始，Codex 很可能能够自行理解已有组件；如果从头开始，最好明确说明。</p> <p>要求 Codex 把截图作为视觉目标，同时将其转化为项目实际采用的工具类、组件封装、颜色系统、字体比例、间距令牌、路由、状态管理和数据获取模式。</p> <p><a id=\"leverage-playwright\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"playwright\">使用 Playwright</h2> <p>Playwright 很适合帮助 Codex 持续迭代。它可以在真实浏览器中打开应用，将实现与截图进行比较，并根据外观或行为不断调整。</p> <p>它还可以调整浏览器窗口尺寸，检查不同断点下的布局。</p> <p>确保 Codex 已启用 Playwright 交互技能。更多信息请参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/docs/build-skills\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">技能文档（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"iterate\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_10\">迭代</h2> <p>第一版就应该在方向上接近截图。面对复杂的交互或动画，可能需要多轮调整。</p> <p>请 Codex 对照截图检查实现，不要只问能否构建。如果设计目标与现有实现发生冲突，应优先使用仓库的设计系统令牌，并且只进行保持整体风格所需的最小间距或尺寸调整。</p> <p>如果还有当前参考图无法说明的状态，可以补充截图或简短说明。</p> <p><a id=\"suggested-follow-up-prompt\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">建议的后续提示词</h3> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[current implementation image] [reference image]\n\nThis doesn't look right. Make sure to implement something that matches closely the reference:\n\n[if needed, specify what is different]\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">[current implementation image] [reference image]\n\n目前看起来不对。请确保实现效果与参考图高度接近：\n\n[if needed, specify what is different]</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"build-responsive-front-end-designs\">Build responsive front-end designs</h1> <p>Turn screenshots and visual references into responsive UI with visual checks.</p> <p>Use Codex to translate screenshots and design briefs into code that matches the repo's design system, then use Playwright to compare the implementation to your references for different screen sizes and iterate until it looks right.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Creating new front-end projects from scratch</li> <li>Implementing already designed screens or flows from screenshots in an existing codebase</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"skills-plugins\">Skills &amp; Plugins</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Skill or plugin</th> <th>Why use it</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/skills/tree/main/skills/.curated/playwright-interactive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">$playwright (online)</a></td> <td>Open the app in a real browser to verify the implementation and iterate on layout and behavior.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Build responsive front-end designs</p> <p>Suggested reasoning effort：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Implement this UI in the current project using the screenshots and notes I provide as the source of truth.\n\nRequirements:\n- Reuse the existing design system components and tokens.\n- Translate the screenshots into this repo's utilities and component patterns instead of inventing a parallel system.\n- Match spacing, layout, hierarchy, and responsive behavior closely.\n- Respect the repo's routing, state, and data-fetch patterns.\n- Make the page responsive on desktop and mobile.\n- If any screenshot detail is ambiguous, choose the simplest implementation that still matches the overall direction and note the assumption briefly.\n\nValidation:\n- Compare the finished UI against the provided screenshots for both look and behavior.\n- Use $playwright-interactive to check that the UI matches the references and iterate as needed until it does.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">Skills</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"introduction\">Introduction</h2> <p>When you have screenshots, a short design brief, or a few references for inspiration, Codex can turn those into responsive UI without ignoring the patterns already established in your project.</p> <p>With the Playwright skill, Codex can open the app in a real browser, compare the implementation to your screenshots for different screen sizes, and iterate on layout or behavior until the result is closer to the target.</p> <h2 id=\"start-from-references\">Start from references</h2> <p>Give Codex the clearest references you have for the UI you want. A single screenshot can be enough for a narrow task, but the handoff gets better when you include multiple states such as desktop and mobile layouts, hover or selected states, and any empty or loading views that matter.</p> <p>The references do not need to be perfect design assets. They just need to make the intended hierarchy, spacing, and direction concrete enough that Codex is not guessing.</p> <h2 id=\"be-specific\">Be specific</h2> <p>The more specific you are about the expected interaction patterns and the style you want, the better the result will be. The model tends to default to high-frequency patterns and style so if it's not clear from your references that you want something else, the UI might look generic. The more input you give, be it more reference inspiration or more specific instructions, the more you can expect to have a UI that stands out.</p> <h2 id=\"prepare-the-design-system\">Prepare the design system</h2> <p>Codex works best when the target repo already has a clear component layer. Codex can automatically use your existing component and design system instead of recreating them from scratch.</p> <p>If necessary, such as when you're not using a standard stack, specify to Codex which primitives to reuse, where your tokens live, and what the repo considers canonical for buttons, inputs, cards, typography, and icons.</p> <p>If you're starting from an existing codebase, Codex will likely understand on its own how to use your components and design system, but if starting from scratch, it's a good idea to be explicit.</p> <p>Ask Codex to treat the screenshots as a visual target but to translate that target into the project's actual utilities, component wrappers, color system, typography scale, spacing tokens, routing, state management, and data-fetch patterns.</p> <h2 id=\"leverage-playwright\">Leverage Playwright</h2> <p>Playwright is a great tool to help Codex iterate on the UI. With it, Codex can open the app in a real browser, compare the implementation to the screenshots you provided, and iterate on layout or behavior.</p> <p>It can resize the browser window to different screen sizes and check the layout at different breakpoints.</p> <p>Make sure you have the Playwright interactive skill enabled in Codex. For more details, see the <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/docs/build-skills\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">skills documentation (online)</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"iterate\">Iterate</h2> <p>The first pass should already be directionally close to the screenshots. For complex layouts, interactions, or animation-heavy UI, expect a few rounds of adjustment.</p> <p>Ask Codex to compare the implementation back to the screenshots, not just whether the page builds. When conflicts come up, it should prefer the repo's design-system tokens and only make minimal spacing or sizing adjustments needed to preserve the overall look of the design.</p> <p>Use additional screenshots or short notes if they help clarify states that are not clear from one image.</p> <h3 id=\"suggested-follow-up-prompt\">Suggested follow-up prompt</h3> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[current implementation image] [reference image]\n\nThis doesn't look right. Make sure to implement something that matches closely the reference:\n\n[if needed, specify what is different]\n</code></pre>"};
